# Kept out of the distributed archive.
#
# Entries are exact relative file paths -- modkit matches them literally, so
# a bare "tests/" silently does nothing. Add the file, not the directory.

# The suite requires engine modules (tests.modkit, src.core.Data), which is a
# private require against the shipped package, and `pack` treats warnings as
# fatal.
tests/rby_mmo_test.lua

# Test-fixture data, not mod content: the shared HMAC vectors that pin the Lua
# client's auth answer against the Node hub's. Only tests/rby_mmo_test.lua
# loads it, and that file is ignored one line above -- shipping the vectors
# without the suite that reads them puts a table nothing in the archive can
# open in front of a player.
tests/fixtures/hmac_vectors.lua

# The end-to-end drivers reach engine test infrastructure
# (tests/drivers/util.lua) and only mean anything inside a checkout.
tests/drivers/mmo_util.lua
tests/drivers/mmo_host.lua
tests/drivers/mmo_join.lua
tests/drivers/run-mmo-e2e.sh
# The dedicated-hub scenario: the same two reasons, plus a wrapper that shells
# out to node. Listed by name because a bare tests/drivers/ line would do
# nothing -- see the note at the top of this file.
tests/drivers/mmo_guest.lua
tests/drivers/run-hub-e2e.sh
# The co-op scenario: four instances driven through a 2-on-2 battle. Same two
# reasons as every driver above -- they reach tests/drivers/util.lua and mean
# nothing outside a checkout. Both arrived with co-op battles and missed this
# list, so `pack` put them in the v0.7.1 archive; it is the same trap
# my-profile.png and rank.png document further down, and nothing mechanical
# catches it, which is why each driver is named here one per line.
tests/drivers/mmo_quad.lua
tests/drivers/run-quad-e2e.sh
# The turn machine's cross-runtime parity pair. The driver is standalone Lua --
# it reaches no engine module, so it would pack clean -- but it exists only to
# be spawned by server/battle_turn.test.js, and its JSON is that spawn's stand-in
# for a checkout with no luajit. Shipping either to a player puts a file nothing
# in the archive opens in front of them, which is the same reason
# tests/fixtures/hmac_vectors.lua is listed above.
tests/fixtures/battle_turn_parity.json
tests/fixtures/battle_sim_vectors.json
tests/battle_sim_vectors.lua
tests/battle_sim_turn.lua
tests/hub_battle.lua
tests/mediated_battle_client.lua
tests/coop_mediated.lua
tests/drivers/battle_turn_parity.lua

# The solo-battle work. The two suites stand alone (their own need() resolver,
# no engine reach) but they are still test code, and this file's rule is that
# a player's archive carries the mod and nothing else -- the suites that prove
# it belong to the checkout. The driver pair goes for the older reason as
# well: it reaches tests/drivers/util.lua and means nothing outside a repo.
tests/solo_battle.lua
tests/solo_brain.lua
tests/drivers/solo_battle_e2e.lua
tests/drivers/run-solo-battle-e2e.sh

# Working notes for whoever picks this up next; not mod content. The two
# machine-readable ones describe how coding agents are driven in this
# checkout -- they configure a tool, not a game, and a player who unpacks the
# archive has no use for either.
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS_CONFIG.yml
skills-lock.json

# server/ is deliberately NOT ignored: hub.js is plain node with no engine
# imports, and someone who installs the package should be able to host.
#
# That reasoning now covers more files, and it still holds for all of them.
# server/bin/, server/lib/, server/package.json, server/Dockerfile and
# server/compose.yml stay in the archive for exactly the reason hub.js does:
# the person who unpacks this should be able to host a world without going
# back to GitHub for the missing half. `docker compose up` is only a complete
# answer if the compose file, the Dockerfile it builds and the package.json
# they describe all travel together -- ignoring any one of them would ship a
# compose file that cannot build. They are inert to the game client, which
# never opens anything under server/, and they carry no ROM-derived bytes, so
# neither `lint` nor the fatal-warning `pack` gate has anything to say about
# them.
#
# server/.dockerignore needs no entry and must not get one: modkit skips every
# dot-prefixed file when it walks the tree (tools/modkit.py:174-176), so it
# cannot be packed in the first place. The same is true of the .env and
# .github entries below, which are kept only as documentation of intent.
#
# server/*.test.js is likewise left shipping, unchanged from before: hub.test.js
# drives the hub over real sockets, and auth/cli/config/limits/server.test.js
# cover the modules beside them. Every one of them requires only ./lib/* and
# node core -- nothing from the engine, which is what earns
# `tests/rby_mmo_test.lua` its entry above and not these. `npm test` is
# `node --test`, which discovers all six, so shipping them is what makes
# "check the copy you just unpacked" a command a host can actually run.

# tools/play.sh and tools/env.sh assume the engine checkout layout, and
# .env is machine-specific -- neither belongs in a distributed archive.
tools/env.sh
tools/play.sh
# Regenerates assets/fonts/battle_hud.png; players need the PNG, not Pillow.
tools/gen_battle_hud_font.py
.env
.env.example

